It's easy to become lazy about doing the same thing.

1522
00:52:06,986 --> 00:52:11,096
I can feel that my body feels different from not doing it.

1523
00:52:12,526 --> 00:52:14,726
(His simple morning workout routine is over.)

1524
00:52:16,396 --> 00:52:20,436
(I should wash up quickly.)

1525
00:52:21,936 --> 00:52:24,036
(He chooses his outfit after washing up quickly.)

1526
00:52:25,806 --> 00:52:27,906
- What? Hold on. - It's a suit?

1527
00:52:27,976 --> 00:52:29,406
It feels like he's going to work.

1528
00:52:29,446 --> 00:52:31,146
It's different from his usual style.

1529
00:52:31,246 --> 00:52:32,345
Right. It's different.

1530
00:52:32,346 --> 00:52:33,576
(A neat look unlike last time)

1531
00:52:34,686 --> 00:52:36,386
(He then opens the fridge.)

1532
00:52:37,146 --> 00:52:38,185
(It's quite packed.)

1533
00:52:38,186 --> 00:52:39,656
- Gosh. - A lot of things were bought.

1534
00:52:40,916 --> 00:52:42,326
(Rummaging)

1535
00:52:42,956 --> 00:52:45,096
(Grapefruit and apple)

1536
00:52:46,026 --> 00:52:47,926
(A pack of yogurt as well)

1537
00:52:50,796 --> 00:52:52,966
(He hurriedly eats while standing.)

1538
00:52:55,306 --> 00:52:57,506
(He also eats the apple while standing.)

1539
00:52:58,236 --> 00:53:01,546
I was in a hurry, so I couldn't even sit.

1540
00:53:02,146 --> 00:53:03,546
He feels like an office worker.

1541
00:53:05,546 --> 00:53:08,486
(He quickly finishes his meal while standing.)

1542
00:53:09,846 --> 00:53:11,356
(Rustling)

1543
00:53:12,886 --> 00:53:14,055
The food waste.

1544
00:53:14,056 --> 00:53:15,986
(He cleans up the food waste right away as well.)

1545
00:53:20,026 --> 00:53:22,395
(Opening)

1546
00:53:22,396 --> 00:53:23,666
The freezer.

1547
00:53:23,826 --> 00:53:25,665
Gosh, he really has adjusted.

1548
00:53:25,666 --> 00:53:27,266
It's put in the freezer.

1549
00:53:27,866 --> 00:53:29,766
The food waste can be thrown out however you want in the US, right?

1550
00:53:30,336 --> 00:53:31,876
It isn't like that.

1551
00:53:32,536 --> 00:53:34,105
- Sorry. - Do you know what it's like there?

1552
00:53:34,106 --> 00:53:35,946
- Do you know what it's like there? - I haven't been to the US.

1553
00:53:36,476 --> 00:53:38,075
I bought one of those.

1554
00:53:38,076 --> 00:53:39,575
- It's the... - The food waste disposer?

1555
00:53:39,576 --> 00:53:42,016
- food waste disposer. - That's the best.

1556
00:53:42,116 --> 00:53:45,215
- You should get him one. - Right.

1557
00:53:45,216 --> 00:53:46,386
Should I?

1558
00:53:47,256 --> 00:53:49,355
It's all for your good.

1559
00:53:49,356 --> 00:53:51,295
Got it.

1560
00:53:51,296 --> 00:53:52,955
- What's the point of just talking? - Got it.

1561
00:53:52,956 --> 00:53:55,325
- Right. - Send one to his place right away.

1562
00:53:55,326 --> 00:53:58,565
Hue seemed to lack a few things.

1563
00:53:58,566 --> 00:54:00,096
- He lives a minimal life. - He's missing a few things.

1564
00:54:00,196 --> 00:54:01,906
I will make sure to fill your house!

1565
00:54:02,736 --> 00:54:04,336
There are a lot of cheaper ones these days.

1566
00:54:06,006 --> 00:54:09,176
(It's kept in the freezer before Mr. Suck-Up gets him one.)

1567
00:54:10,676 --> 00:54:11,816
(Rushing)

1568
00:54:12,476 --> 00:54:15,186
(Let's get going.)

1569
00:54:16,416 --> 00:54:20,016
(He hurriedly leaves the house.)

1570
00:54:24,426 --> 00:54:27,156
- It feels like Korea right away. - It's Korea.

1571
00:54:27,326 --> 00:54:29,326
He feels like an office worker.

1572
00:54:30,826 --> 00:54:32,636
(Rushing)

1573
00:54:34,566 --> 00:54:36,736
(He moves into the crowd.)

1574
00:54:38,106 --> 00:54:40,476
(Okay. I'm good with time.)

1575
00:54:40,576 --> 00:54:41,705
(Yongsan Station)

1576
00:54:41,706 --> 00:54:43,845
(He came to Yongsan Station in the busy morning.)

1577
00:54:43,846 --> 00:54:44,945
Where is he going?

1578
00:54:44,946 --> 00:54:46,775
Yongsan Station is really busy.

1579
00:54:46,776 --> 00:54:48,846
(Crowded)

1580
00:54:50,946 --> 00:54:53,686
(He naturally joins in...)

1581
00:54:54,386 --> 00:54:56,386
(with the people getting to work.)

1582
00:54:57,826 --> 00:55:00,326
Our country's subway system is very convenient, isn't it?

1583
00:55:00,626 --> 00:55:02,895
- And it comes right on time. - Right.

1584
00:55:02,896 --> 00:55:04,636
- It's the best thing to have. - I got really surprised.

1585
00:55:07,036 --> 00:55:09,036
(Rummaging)

1586
00:55:09,936 --> 00:55:13,006
(He suddenly gets out a pen and notebook.)

1587
00:55:15,206 --> 00:55:18,216
(He starts writing something.)

1588
00:55:18,946 --> 00:55:22,146
This is what's good about using public transportation.

1589
00:55:22,786 --> 00:55:24,285
Throughout that long period of time,

1590
00:55:24,286 --> 00:55:27,015
it's nice that you can use it to prepare for something.

1591
00:55:27,016 --> 00:55:28,086
(He spends his time on the subway efficiently.)

1592
00:55:28,726 --> 00:55:30,525
It feels like New York with this scene.

1593
00:55:30,526 --> 00:55:31,526
Right.

1594
00:55:31,896 --> 00:55:34,256
I thought it was quite strange.

1595
00:55:34,496 --> 00:55:37,795
It was because there were a couple of foreigners.

1596
00:55:37,796 --> 00:55:40,665
I was wondering why it felt just like New York.

1597
00:55:40,666 --> 00:55:42,005
"Which one is New York?"

1598
00:55:42,006 --> 00:55:43,006
There are a lot of foreigners in Seoul.

1599
00:55:43,007 --> 00:55:44,635
- "Where is New York?" - The place where Hue Park is.

1600
00:55:44,636 --> 00:55:45,706
Right.

1601
00:55:46,906 --> 00:55:48,346
- What? - Goodness.

1602
00:55:49,246 --> 00:55:50,505
(Unable to believe what they just heard)

1603
00:55:50,506 --> 00:55:52,745
What was that about? "The place where Hue Park is?"

1604
00:55:52,746 --> 00:55:53,746
That is where New York is.

1605
00:55:53,747 --> 00:55:56,785
Mr. Suck-Up is at it again.

1606
00:55:56,786 --> 00:55:58,356
(Mr. Suck-Up is getting more expressive over time.)

1607
00:55:58,686 --> 00:56:00,916
(While Hue is making his surroundings feel like New York, )

1608
00:56:02,026 --> 00:56:04,756
(the subway heads further away.)

1609
00:56:05,256 --> 00:56:06,656
How far is he going?

1610
00:56:08,066 --> 00:56:10,925
This stop is Suwon.

1611
00:56:10,926 --> 00:56:13,735
- Suwon? - He went really far.

1612
00:56:13,736 --> 00:56:16,105
I was on the subway for over an hour.

1613
00:56:16,106 --> 00:56:17,536
- Right. That's far. - Right.

1614
00:56:19,376 --> 00:56:20,905
Gosh, there are a lot of people.

1615
00:56:20,906 --> 00:56:22,776
(He moves quickly in Suwon as well.)

1616
00:56:24,716 --> 00:56:26,276
(Running up)

1617
00:56:27,676 --> 00:56:30,746
(He heads somewhere in hurried steps.)

1618
00:56:32,086 --> 00:56:34,216
- Doesn't it feel like a school? - A school?

1619
00:56:35,421 --> 00:56:38,690
(Hue heads in somewhere after moving a long distance.)

1620
00:56:38,691 --> 00:56:40,790
(Cross-Culture Generation: Global Generation Storytelling)

1621
00:56:40,791 --> 00:56:43,530
(Hue Park)

1622
00:56:43,531 --> 00:56:46,130
Today, I'll be at Kyung Hee University...

1623
00:56:46,131 --> 00:56:48,771
for a lecture for the students.

1624
00:56:52,211 --> 00:56:55,941
I will talk about the things that I do.

1625
00:56:56,641 --> 00:56:59,480
I will be doing a lecture based on...

1626
00:56:59,481 --> 00:57:02,681
my tough times while being a college of arts student.

1627
00:57:03,321 --> 00:57:05,591
- They all want to hear from him. - This is a great time for them.

1628
00:57:05,891 --> 00:57:08,660
It's different when they come with their own dreams.

1629
00:57:08,661 --> 00:57:10,691
Their eyes will be shiny.

1630
00:57:12,831 --> 00:57:14,901
(The lecture hall gets filled as they wait for Hue.)

1631
00:57:16,561 --> 00:57:17,931
The title is all in English.

1632
00:57:18,301 --> 00:57:19,500
Cross-Culture Generation,

1633
00:57:19,501 --> 00:57:21,671
- Global Generation Storytelling. - What does that even mean?

1634
00:57:21,801 --> 00:57:24,141
- It's all in English. - What does that mean?

1635
00:57:24,511 --> 00:57:25,910
- Cross-Culture... - It means, "Let's do this."

1636
00:57:25,911 --> 00:57:26,970
- That's what it means? - "Let's do this."

1637
00:57:26,971 --> 00:57:27,980
"Let's do this."

1638
00:57:27,981 --> 00:57:29,540
How does that mean, "Let's do this?"

1639
00:57:29,541 --> 00:57:30,980
Why does that have to sound so hard?

1640
00:57:30,981 --> 00:57:32,450
You're looking down on us so much.

1641
00:57:32,451 --> 00:57:33,750
(Are you looking down on us?)

1642
00:57:33,751 --> 00:57:35,350
- He's cheering up the next gen. - Is that so?

1643
00:57:35,351 --> 00:57:37,551
- Right. - I see.

1644
00:57:38,121 --> 00:57:40,721
(Hue has come to cheer up the students.)

1645
00:57:42,591 --> 00:57:45,061
(Drinking water)

1646
00:57:46,461 --> 00:57:49,031
(He continues to drink the water.)

1647
00:57:51,731 --> 00:57:54,100
I'm really nervous right now.

1648
00:57:54,101 --> 00:57:55,401
- It seems like it. - Right.

1649
00:57:56,071 --> 00:57:57,570
Out of all lectures,

1650
00:57:57,571 --> 00:58:00,071
lectures for students are the toughest.

1651
00:58:00,511 --> 00:58:02,580
It's because a single thing that you say...

1652
00:58:02,581 --> 00:58:03,910
- is able to... - There's a big responsibility.

1653
00:58:03,911 --> 00:58:05,610
Right. It's able to change their lives.

1654
00:58:05,611 --> 00:58:08,081
That is why you feel hesitant to say things.

1655
00:58:08,381 --> 00:58:10,821
He's a bit like Kai.

1656
00:58:11,091 --> 00:58:13,020
- He's a professor. - Right.

1657
00:58:13,021 --> 00:58:14,351
(Professor Kai feels proud.)

1658
00:58:15,691 --> 00:58:17,390
Everything is ready.

1659
00:58:17,391 --> 00:58:18,760
- You may head in now. - Right.

1660
00:58:18,761 --> 00:58:20,431
- Should I head in now? - Yes.

1661
00:58:21,731 --> 00:58:24,301
(Unlike his voice, his face looks very nervous.)

1662
00:58:26,571 --> 00:58:30,441
(Let's go, Hue!)

1663
00:58:31,671 --> 00:58:33,241
Please give him a warm welcome.

1664
00:58:33,341 --> 00:58:36,410
Here's Writer Hue Park!

1665
00:58:36,411 --> 00:58:38,480
(Here's Writer Hue Park!)

1666
00:58:38,481 --> 00:58:40,550
- There are a lot of them. - There are a lot of them.

1667
00:58:40,551 --> 00:58:41,651
Gosh!

1668
00:58:41,751 --> 00:58:44,451
There were around 300 students.

1669
00:58:45,891 --> 00:58:47,760
(The students welcome him with a round of applause.)

1670
00:58:47,761 --> 00:58:49,621
- A lot of them have come. - Gosh.

1671
00:58:51,191 --> 00:58:53,631
(Smiling)

1672
00:58:55,031 --> 00:58:56,460
(Holding her hands tight)

1673
00:58:56,461 --> 00:58:57,661
This is exciting.

1674
00:58:57,931 --> 00:59:01,340
Hello, everyone. I'm a musical writer,

1675
00:59:01,341 --> 00:59:04,300
lyricist, and director.

1676
00:59:04,301 --> 00:59:06,770
My name is Hue Park. It's a pleasure to meet you.

1677
00:59:06,771 --> 00:59:08,840
(It's a pleasure to meet you.)

1678
00:59:08,841 --> 00:59:10,181
The atmosphere feels great.

1679
00:59:10,841 --> 00:59:12,881
(Feeling relieved by the great atmosphere)

1680
00:59:13,711 --> 00:59:17,220
It's been a while since I visited a school campus,

1681
00:59:17,221 --> 00:59:19,021
and it feels very exciting.

1682
00:59:19,291 --> 00:59:20,891
It reminded me a lot about my past.

1683
00:59:21,321 --> 00:59:23,861
I've been here 20 years ago.

1684
00:59:24,491 --> 00:59:27,391
That was why I felt excited on my way here.

1685
00:59:28,831 --> 00:59:29,831
(Feeling nervous)

1686
00:59:29,832 --> 00:59:34,001
First of all, I have a question for all of you.

1687
00:59:36,741 --> 00:59:37,970
- What? - "After..."

1688
00:59:37,971 --> 00:59:39,041
(He opens up his notebook with his shaky hands.)

1689
00:59:39,611 --> 00:59:41,240
"finishing my studies,"

1690
00:59:41,241 --> 00:59:44,610
"I feel certain about what I will do in the future."

1691
00:59:44,611 --> 00:59:45,881
Can I see a raise of hands for those who feel this way?

1692
00:59:46,651 --> 00:59:48,751
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight...

1693
00:59:49,381 --> 00:59:52,951
There seems to be only around ten people.

1694
00:59:54,221 --> 00:59:56,290
I think I've done a good job to come here.

1695
00:59:56,291 --> 01:00:00,131
It's because I was just the same...

1696
01:00:00,291 --> 01:00:01,960
as those who didn't raise their hands.

1697
01:00:01,961 --> 01:00:03,831
(I didn't feel certain about my future, like most students.)

1698
01:00:03,961 --> 01:00:07,631
I've changed my major twice.

1699
01:00:07,871 --> 01:00:10,070
I've attended two schools.

1700
01:00:10,071 --> 01:00:14,241
In both schools, I felt that I was someone...

1701
01:00:14,941 --> 01:00:16,941
that was quite distant from the mainstream.

1702
01:00:17,811 --> 01:00:22,221
"Where will a person like me feel welcomed?"

1703
01:00:22,651 --> 01:00:24,751
"Why am I trying to become an artist?"

1704
01:00:25,091 --> 01:00:28,490
I really struggled with these thoughts...

1705
01:00:28,491 --> 01:00:30,491
during my 20s.

1706
01:00:31,091 --> 01:00:35,131
I'm saying this for those who might be thinking the same.

1707
01:00:35,661 --> 01:00:38,031
I wanted to tell you...

1708
01:00:38,331 --> 01:00:41,241
that I felt lost for a long time...

1709
01:00:41,471 --> 01:00:43,071
like all of you.

1710
01:00:44,071 --> 01:00:45,810
I graduated from an art high school...

1711
01:00:45,811 --> 01:00:47,140
and entered an art university.

1712
01:00:47,141 --> 01:00:50,010
It was really hard to enter,

1713
01:00:50,011 --> 01:00:52,380
but once I got in, I felt lost about...

1714
01:00:52,381 --> 01:00:54,310
what I was going to make a living with.

1715
01:00:54,311 --> 01:00:56,320
- That's how it feels in that field. - It feels vague.

1716
01:00:56,321 --> 01:00:57,321
Right.

1717
01:00:57,322 --> 01:01:00,290
But when a successful senior...

1718
01:01:00,291 --> 01:01:02,790
who studied the same major says something like that,

1719
01:01:02,791 --> 01:01:04,290
- you would feel very grateful. - Right.

1720
01:01:04,291 --> 01:01:05,460
- Seriously. - You would feel hope.

1721
01:01:05,461 --> 01:01:09,161
Your own tastes are very important.

1722
01:01:09,601 --> 01:01:11,671
No matter how pathetic people say you are,

1723
01:01:11,901 --> 01:01:13,830
you must know what your tastes are...

1724
01:01:13,831 --> 01:01:15,841
and continue to develop them.

1725
01:01:16,171 --> 01:01:17,540
I don't think following a trend...

1726
01:01:17,541 --> 01:01:19,411
is what an artist does.

1727
01:01:21,781 --> 01:01:22,781
My gosh!

1728
01:01:22,782 --> 01:01:24,181
You're definitely not an artist.

1729
01:01:24,381 --> 01:01:26,611
(The trend hunter suddenly makes an appearance.)

1730
01:01:27,011 --> 01:01:28,621
I've never been surprised before after seeing my face.

1731
01:01:28,851 --> 01:01:30,480
- What was that? - Well,

1732
01:01:30,481 --> 01:01:32,020
I see that as a form of art in this generation.

1733
01:01:32,021 --> 01:01:33,091
Right.

1734
01:01:34,051 --> 01:01:36,460
(Maesaengi Art, Jun Hyun Moo, 2025)

1735
01:01:36,461 --> 01:01:38,390
The sense of emptiness from trends.

1736
01:01:38,391 --> 01:01:39,761
That was what I expressed.

1737
01:01:40,531 --> 01:01:41,830
I don't think following a trend...

1738
01:01:41,831 --> 01:01:43,701
is what an artist does.

1739
01:01:43,831 --> 01:01:46,231
A person who endlessly thinks about their philosophy...

1740
01:01:46,471 --> 01:01:48,940
and creates a trend.

1741
01:01:48,941 --> 01:01:50,140
- That is an artist. - The one who creates a trend.

1742
01:01:50,141 --> 01:01:54,041
You must not become people who follow trends.

1743
01:01:54,681 --> 01:01:57,380
I think that is what an artist must do.

1744
01:01:57,381 --> 01:01:59,150
I did a lecture for about 40 minutes...

1745
01:01:59,151 --> 01:02:01,451
while explaining my timeline.

1746
01:02:02,381 --> 01:02:05,091
I wandered around here and there and spat out a lot of things.

1747
01:02:05,251 --> 01:02:06,651
I'll stop speaking now.

1748
01:02:06,921 --> 01:02:08,461
Since I'm here,

1749
01:02:08,661 --> 01:02:11,120
feel free to ask me questions.

1750
01:02:11,121 --> 01:02:13,291
- The Q and A session. - That's the best part.

1751
01:02:13,961 --> 01:02:15,830
Shall we use this time for some questions?

1752
01:02:15,831 --> 01:02:18,400
Do you have any questions for me?

1753
01:02:18,401 --> 01:02:20,070
(Raising their hands right away)

1754
01:02:20,071 --> 01:02:21,230
- They responded right away. - I got so surprised.

1755
01:02:21,231 --> 01:02:22,440
- Many of them raised their hands. - Right.

1756
01:02:22,441 --> 01:02:24,171
That means they enjoyed the lecture.

1757
01:02:25,511 --> 01:02:26,910
(Beaming)

1758
01:02:26,911 --> 01:02:28,011
Yes.

1759
01:02:28,481 --> 01:02:30,281
The student in the black outfit.

1760
01:02:31,281 --> 01:02:35,051
I heard you worked as a designer in an advertising agency...

1761
01:02:35,181 --> 01:02:37,451
and wrote at night.

1762
01:02:38,021 --> 01:02:41,320
I want to ask if you had any difficulties...

1763
01:02:41,321 --> 01:02:45,331
while making money and being an artist.

1764
01:02:45,461 --> 01:02:47,760
Well, it's the same for everyone.

1765
01:02:47,761 --> 01:02:50,100
We were all freelancers too.

1766
01:02:50,101 --> 01:02:52,670
There's nothing that can be done before you become famous.

1767
01:02:52,671 --> 01:02:55,000
You aren't able to make money with what you enjoy doing.

1768
01:02:55,001 --> 01:02:56,970
You can't sit around and do nothing.

1769
01:02:56,971 --> 01:02:58,011
Right.

1770
01:02:58,171 --> 01:02:59,671
Did you just answer her question?

1771
01:03:00,011 --> 01:03:01,740
- "Nothing can be done." - It's the same for everyone.

1772
01:03:01,741 --> 01:03:03,810
(She answered the younger one's question right away.)

1773
01:03:03,811 --> 01:03:07,150
You have to have two jobs. What else can you do?

1774
01:03:07,151 --> 01:03:08,220
That was a great answer.

1775
01:03:08,221 --> 01:03:09,620
You should do lectures as well.

1776
01:03:09,621 --> 01:03:11,050
- She's so straightforward. - Go for it.

1777
01:03:11,051 --> 01:03:12,290
- You should do lectures as well. - But...

1778
01:03:12,291 --> 01:03:13,650
people need to hear things like this as well.

1779
01:03:13,651 --> 01:03:15,091
- Right. - There's nothing you can do.

1780
01:03:16,421 --> 01:03:18,590
It's definitely hard to do your work...

1781
01:03:18,591 --> 01:03:21,331
while working at a company.

1782
01:03:21,561 --> 01:03:24,730
You should make sure to draw a line.

1783
01:03:24,731 --> 01:03:26,100
When you are at work,

1784
01:03:26,101 --> 01:03:27,970
you respect the tastes of the clients...

1785
01:03:27,971 --> 01:03:30,201
and respect the needs of your superiors.

1786
01:03:30,801 --> 01:03:32,740
When it was my day off or when I came back home,

1787
01:03:32,741 --> 01:03:35,110
I tried hard to respect my own tastes.

1788
01:03:35,111 --> 01:03:36,311
That's the right thing to do.

1789
01:03:37,881 --> 01:03:39,911
The one wearing the hoodie.

1790
01:03:40,251 --> 01:03:44,150
How would you define "good lyrics?"

1791
01:03:44,151 --> 01:03:47,450
How would you define that person?

1792
01:03:47,451 --> 01:03:49,421
I would like to ask how you get inspired...

1793
01:03:49,791 --> 01:03:52,891
to create your work.

1794
01:03:53,291 --> 01:03:57,600
I read that inspiration always comes at a specific time.

1795
01:03:57,601 --> 01:04:00,070
There is someone who said...

1796
01:04:00,071 --> 01:04:02,400
that it's a matter of whether you're sitting at your desk or not.

1797
01:04:02,401 --> 01:04:05,341
I read that from another writer.

1798
01:04:06,441 --> 01:04:08,340
(They work hard to take notes so they don't miss anything.)

1799
01:04:08,341 --> 01:04:09,481
They're taking notes as well.

1800
01:04:10,381 --> 01:04:13,810
It's very sad to let him go, but we must end things here.

1801
01:04:13,811 --> 01:04:16,080
Please give him another big round of applause.

1802
01:04:16,081 --> 01:04:18,491
Thank you, everyone!

1803
01:04:19,491 --> 01:04:21,050
They look very satisfied.

1804
01:04:21,051 --> 01:04:22,161
They enjoyed it.

1805
01:04:23,891 --> 01:04:25,260
(The lecture ends in great success.)

1806
01:04:25,261 --> 01:04:27,791
- Thank you for your effort. - Thank you.

1807
01:04:30,001 --> 01:04:32,801
(He heads to the lobby right after the lecture.)

1808
01:04:33,201 --> 01:04:34,271
Gosh!

1809
01:04:36,741 --> 01:04:39,140
(Please give us your autograph.)

1810
01:04:39,141 --> 01:04:41,041
- They all wanted an autograph? - As expected.

1811
01:04:41,581 --> 01:04:43,341
(Crowded)

1812
01:04:44,611 --> 01:04:46,751
(After the lecture, )

1813
01:04:46,951 --> 01:04:49,681
(the students all gathered in the lobby.)

1814
01:04:50,621 --> 01:04:52,991
(It suddenly feels like a fan autograph event.)

1815
01:04:53,451 --> 01:04:55,961
- What is your name? - I'm Jang Se Rin.

1816
01:04:56,661 --> 01:04:57,891
Thank you.

1817
01:04:58,261 --> 01:04:59,331
Right.

1818
01:05:01,661 --> 01:05:03,500
- Thank you. - I remember the time in New York.

1819
01:05:03,501 --> 01:05:05,371
When the actors and actresses left the theater,

1820
01:05:05,871 --> 01:05:07,230
- they signed autographs. - Right.

1821
01:05:07,231 --> 01:05:09,741
I remember when you quietly got out while they did that.

1822
01:05:11,071 --> 01:05:13,011
- I'm Jang Soo Min. - Soo Min?

1823
01:05:13,311 --> 01:05:14,840
To be honest, I'm not used to it.

1824
01:05:14,841 --> 01:05:17,281
It still feels awkward to sign autographs for someone.

1825
01:05:17,441 --> 01:05:19,150
Asking for my autograph...

1826
01:05:19,151 --> 01:05:23,381
makes me think that my lecture was meaningful.

1827
01:05:25,321 --> 01:05:26,621
I really enjoyed your shows.

1828
01:05:27,121 --> 01:05:28,890
I watched "Il Tenore" and "Maybe Happy Ending..."

1829
01:05:28,891 --> 01:05:30,391
and I cried during both of them.

1830
01:05:31,421 --> 01:05:33,991
I'll make sure to watch the show that will be out soon.

1831
01:05:34,631 --> 01:05:35,701
Thank you.

1832
01:05:35,801 --> 01:05:37,860
- This is for you. - Thank you.

1833
01:05:37,861 --> 01:05:39,370
I did eat,

1834
01:05:39,371 --> 01:05:40,530
- but I'll make sure to enjoy this. - Really?

1835
01:05:40,531 --> 01:05:41,540
Thank you.

1836
01:05:41,541 --> 01:05:43,901
- This is such a precious thing. - It's for you.

1837
01:05:44,141 --> 01:05:45,640
- Thank you. - It's a Labubu.

1838
01:05:45,641 --> 01:05:46,770
(Touched by their warmth from their gifts)

1839
01:05:46,771 --> 01:05:48,210
It should be checked if it's a fake one.

1840
01:05:48,211 --> 01:05:49,640
(Being jealous)

1841
01:05:49,641 --> 01:05:51,550
- Thank you! - Gosh, they're going wild.

1842
01:05:51,551 --> 01:05:53,010
(The students feel delighted by his heartfelt autograph.)

1843
01:05:53,011 --> 01:05:54,251
They look delighted.

1844
01:05:55,081 --> 01:05:58,690
I met the young students and they felt so cute and lovely.

1845
01:05:58,691 --> 01:06:00,551
They felt like babies to me.

1846
01:06:00,721 --> 01:06:02,790
Had I gotten married with my first love...

1847
01:06:02,791 --> 01:06:04,490
during my college days,

1848
01:06:04,491 --> 01:06:07,791
they would've been around the age of my son or daughter.

1849
01:06:08,061 --> 01:06:09,500
When I was around their age,

1850
01:06:09,501 --> 01:06:16,271
I used to transcribe Writer Noh Hee Kyung's scripts.

1851
01:06:16,701 --> 01:06:20,411
I went to the university campus that made me feel excited...

1852
01:06:20,971 --> 01:06:23,411
and I got so much energy from the students.

1853
01:06:23,841 --> 01:06:25,910
- I'm a big fan. - Thank you.

1854
01:06:25,911 --> 01:06:27,210
You did it for all of them?

1855
01:06:27,211 --> 01:06:29,481
- I'm a big fan. - Good luck with your work.

1856
01:06:30,021 --> 01:06:31,321
Thank you.

1857
01:06:31,791 --> 01:06:32,791
Thank you.

1858
01:06:33,191 --> 01:06:36,561
Actually, there was a question I wanted to ask.

1859
01:06:36,991 --> 01:06:39,161
You mentioned personal preferences earlier.

1860
01:06:39,461 --> 01:06:42,160
I wondered if I should try different things even if I don't like them...

1861
01:06:42,161 --> 01:06:43,831
- when I'm still in school. - Of course.

1862
01:06:44,531 --> 01:06:47,731
You should go to a gallery every week.

1863
01:06:48,131 --> 01:06:49,370
Check out nice design blogs.

1864
01:06:49,371 --> 01:06:51,470
Check out blogs like It's Nice That every day.

1865
01:06:51,471 --> 01:06:53,611
She studies visual design.

1866
01:06:54,441 --> 01:06:56,811
You should check out other people's work when you're a student...

1867
01:06:57,341 --> 01:06:58,580
even if it's not what you prefer.

1868
01:06:58,581 --> 01:07:00,581
Try things that aren't yours.

1869
01:07:00,781 --> 01:07:02,051
Try imitating others too.

1870
01:07:02,321 --> 01:07:04,991
Read philosophy books even if it's not your major.

1871
01:07:05,121 --> 01:07:07,950
See how the world works through newspapers.

1872
01:07:07,951 --> 01:07:09,160
Watch news as well.

1873
01:07:09,161 --> 01:07:11,420
The things you thought were never your taste...

1874
01:07:11,421 --> 01:07:13,361
somehow become your taste one day.

1875
01:07:13,661 --> 01:07:14,760
You should try everything...

1876
01:07:14,761 --> 01:07:15,831
- at that time. - That's right.

1877
01:07:16,431 --> 01:07:18,130
- Thank you. - Study hard.

1878
01:07:18,131 --> 01:07:19,371
(Hue answers even the last person's question with sincerity.)

1879
01:07:20,571 --> 01:07:21,941
Gosh, it's fall.

1880
01:07:22,071 --> 01:07:23,670
- Goodness. - Gosh, the campus is pretty.

1881
01:07:23,671 --> 01:07:24,671
Campuses are the best in fall.

1882
01:07:24,672 --> 01:07:28,111
I think the air on campus is different from outside.

1883
01:07:29,181 --> 01:07:30,241
Gosh, I feel so relieved.

1884
01:07:32,151 --> 01:07:33,380
- Thank you! - Thank you.

1885
01:07:33,381 --> 01:07:34,481
- Sure. - Thank you.

1886
01:07:37,221 --> 01:07:38,291
Bye.

1887
01:07:38,821 --> 01:07:41,391
- It's nice to see college students. - It gives you energy.

1888
01:07:41,721 --> 01:07:44,291
They really felt like my nephews and nieces.

1889
01:07:44,761 --> 01:07:47,230
And you'll meet one of them later in life.

1890
01:07:47,231 --> 01:07:48,700
- Right. - Actually, that's what they said.

1891
01:07:48,701 --> 01:07:50,660
"Let's meet on site."

1892
01:07:50,661 --> 01:07:52,500
- Such things are bound to happen. - That's right.

1893
01:07:52,501 --> 01:07:54,531
- "I was in that lecture hall." - Exactly.

1894
01:07:54,701 --> 01:07:55,740
Then you'd meet them.

1895
01:07:55,741 --> 01:07:57,141
It'd be really fascinating.

1896
01:07:59,611 --> 01:08:00,811
The weather is amazing.

1897
01:08:05,111 --> 01:08:06,410
(He finally sees the fall scenery after his tension eased.)

1898
01:08:06,411 --> 01:08:07,551
Look at the weather.

1899
01:08:09,481 --> 01:08:11,021
You're going to Seoul right away?

1900
01:08:11,321 --> 01:08:12,451
Yes, I had to...

1901
01:08:14,091 --> 01:08:15,421
What? He didn't go home.

1902
01:08:16,091 --> 01:08:17,961
- There were foreigners everywhere. - I know.

1903
01:08:18,461 --> 01:08:19,691
It feels like New York.

1904
01:08:19,961 --> 01:08:21,561
I feel like I'm seeing more than on "Surprise Mystery TV."

1905
01:08:22,401 --> 01:08:23,800
- Behind him... - There are definitely more...

1906
01:08:23,801 --> 01:08:25,200
- these days. - There are a lot these days.

1907
01:08:25,201 --> 01:08:26,600
- How can there be foreigners... - There are a lot.

1908
01:08:26,601 --> 01:08:28,000
- behind and next to him? - There are so many.

1909
01:08:28,001 --> 01:08:31,001
How is it possible when it's not even Itaewon Station?

1910
01:08:35,411 --> 01:08:36,581
Where did he go?

1911
01:08:37,381 --> 01:08:38,481
A cafe?

1912
01:08:40,951 --> 01:08:41,980
(Aladdin Used Bookstore)

1913
01:08:41,981 --> 01:08:43,381
Oh, it's Aladdin.

1914
01:08:43,881 --> 01:08:44,951
Aladdin?

1915
01:08:49,461 --> 01:08:52,661
It's a used bookstore I used to go often.

1916
01:08:54,161 --> 01:08:56,560
I used to go there during lunch break after having lunch...

1917
01:08:56,561 --> 01:08:58,671
and read books.

1918
01:08:59,571 --> 01:09:01,901
- "Books there were just sold." - Gosh, the books.

1919
01:09:02,771 --> 01:09:05,341
- Isn't that on Garosu-gil? - That's right.

1920
01:09:05,641 --> 01:09:07,241
I sold a lot of books there.

1921
01:09:07,411 --> 01:09:09,510
- When? - Then I might have your books.

1922
01:09:09,511 --> 01:09:11,040
You might. It feels really good when you earn two dollars...

1923
01:09:11,041 --> 01:09:12,551
after selling a book.

1924
01:09:12,681 --> 01:09:14,111
You went to sell your books, not buy?

1925
01:09:14,451 --> 01:09:15,651
I've never bought books from there.

1926
01:09:17,781 --> 01:09:19,450
You're so generous. You pay more for new books...

1927
01:09:19,451 --> 01:09:21,961
- and sell them cheap. - I drive the economy.

1928
01:09:22,521 --> 01:09:24,621
They pay you 3 dollars for books that cost 20 dollars.

1929
01:09:25,031 --> 01:09:27,130
- It's really infuriating. - He drives the economy?

1930
01:09:27,131 --> 01:09:29,260
- That'd be really neat. - To make money for valet parking.

1931
01:09:29,261 --> 01:09:30,901
There are so many people who sell books there.

1932
01:09:31,001 --> 01:09:32,500
Someone needs to sell their books...

1933
01:09:32,501 --> 01:09:34,530
- for others to buy them. - Mine were completely new.

1934
01:09:34,531 --> 01:09:35,571
I'd never even touched them.

1935
01:09:36,141 --> 01:09:38,270
- Completely new. - I see.

1936
01:09:38,271 --> 01:09:39,370
There are so many books...

1937
01:09:39,371 --> 01:09:40,511
- in great conditions. - Those are mine.

1938
01:09:41,241 --> 01:09:42,981
- They're really mine. - The good ones.

1939
01:09:43,541 --> 01:09:45,581
(Looking intently)

1940
01:09:47,111 --> 01:09:48,951
("Atonement")

1941
01:09:52,251 --> 01:09:55,920
Actually, just because books are a little worn...

1942
01:09:55,921 --> 01:09:59,261
doesn't mean they depreciate in value.

1943
01:09:59,361 --> 01:10:02,430
On top of that, they have books that went out of print.

1944
01:10:02,431 --> 01:10:05,131
There's a book that went out of print among the books I got today.

1945
01:10:05,301 --> 01:10:08,170
I think it's meaningful to get a book...

1946
01:10:08,171 --> 01:10:10,471
that holds someone's history.

1947
01:10:13,441 --> 01:10:16,240
It's fun to read sentences that are underlined.

1948
01:10:16,241 --> 01:10:17,310
It must be.

1949
01:10:17,311 --> 01:10:20,080
- Oh, you get such books there. - If you get books from there...

1950
01:10:20,081 --> 01:10:21,211
- The books with underlines. - Yes.

1951
01:10:21,381 --> 01:10:25,290
"Why did this sentence resonate with this person?"

1952
01:10:25,291 --> 01:10:27,020
It'd make me think about such things.

1953
01:10:27,021 --> 01:10:28,220
There's that kind of fun.

1954
01:10:28,221 --> 01:10:30,820
The moment you underline, the price plummets.

1955
01:10:30,821 --> 01:10:31,890
They check those?

1956
01:10:31,891 --> 01:10:33,090
They check everything.

1957
01:10:33,091 --> 01:10:34,161
- They do. - Really?

1958
01:10:35,091 --> 01:10:36,901
- What about dog-earing? - No.

1959
01:10:37,331 --> 01:10:38,671
That's not good. The price plummets.

1960
01:10:38,771 --> 01:10:41,271
Also, if there's a trace of food...

1961
01:10:41,371 --> 01:10:42,740
- Iffy ones like kimchi juice. - Yes.

1962
01:10:42,741 --> 01:10:43,770
Oh, if it's dirty?

1963
01:10:43,771 --> 01:10:45,100
- If it's dirty... - Then they don't accept it.

1964
01:10:45,101 --> 01:10:46,110
- They don't accept it? - They don't accept it.

1965
01:10:46,111 --> 01:10:47,340
They don't accept dirty ones.

1966
01:10:47,341 --> 01:10:49,141
My books have many pages with kimchi juice.

1967
01:10:49,581 --> 01:10:51,681
That's because you use them as a placemat.

1968
01:10:52,151 --> 01:10:53,980
- A placemat? - They don't accept one with a drop.

1969
01:10:53,981 --> 01:10:56,050
- They don't? - It was a big bummer.

1970
01:10:56,051 --> 01:10:57,520
If they don't find spots while flipping the pages,

1971
01:10:57,521 --> 01:10:58,590
they'd let it slide?

1972
01:10:58,591 --> 01:11:00,291
But they flip the pages ten times.

1973
01:11:00,491 --> 01:11:02,621
- "Rustling." - Gosh.

1974
01:11:03,121 --> 01:11:05,060
They'd flip pages like a machine.

1975
01:11:05,061 --> 01:11:06,061
"Rustling."

1976
01:11:06,062 --> 01:11:07,531
- They're different. - Of course.

1977
01:11:07,731 --> 01:11:10,461
- Well, they're there for that. - They're professionals.

1978
01:11:11,971 --> 01:11:13,401
(Staring)

1979
01:11:17,041 --> 01:11:19,440
(He takes a look at its cover...)

1980
01:11:19,441 --> 01:11:21,171
(and takes it as well.)

1981
01:11:21,371 --> 01:11:24,580
I don't really regret...

1982
01:11:24,581 --> 01:11:26,981
even if I buy books on impulse.

1983
01:11:28,211 --> 01:11:30,650
You see? You and I have a similar shopping style.

1984
01:11:30,651 --> 01:11:32,120
I buy many at once like that.

1985
01:11:32,121 --> 01:11:33,251
But he actually reads them.

1986
01:11:34,491 --> 01:11:35,491
I...

1987
01:11:36,261 --> 01:11:38,530
- They're completely new. - They're stuck together.

1988
01:11:38,531 --> 01:11:40,261
- Then you can get a good price. - Is that so?

1989
01:11:41,261 --> 01:11:42,301
Congratulations.

1990
01:11:42,961 --> 01:11:44,630
You should buy new ones and sell them there.

1991
01:11:44,631 --> 01:11:45,931
Yes, you should do that.

1992
01:11:46,371 --> 01:11:47,970
- If you have new books... - Please call me in advance...

1993
01:11:47,971 --> 01:11:49,440
- when you go sell your books. - That's right.

1994
01:11:49,441 --> 01:11:51,071
I'll get new ones and sell them right away.

1995
01:11:51,341 --> 01:11:52,740
You should drive the economy.

1996
01:11:52,741 --> 01:11:53,910
- I will. - Yes, you should drive it a lot.

1997
01:11:53,911 --> 01:11:55,011
I keep it moving big time.

1998
01:11:57,011 --> 01:11:58,140
I'm sorry, but there's a lot.

1999
01:11:58,141 --> 01:11:59,711
(Saving the Moomoo chance for another time)

2000
01:12:01,711 --> 01:12:03,981
(He bought 14 books.)

2001
01:12:05,591 --> 01:12:07,820
- Here you go. Thank you. - Thank you. Bye.

2002
01:12:07,821 --> 01:12:08,960
Goodbye.

2003
01:12:08,961 --> 01:12:10,861
(With both hands full with books)

2004
01:12:12,491 --> 01:12:16,061
(He heads straight home.)

2005
01:12:17,601 --> 01:12:19,370
Gosh, he has to unpack them.

2006
01:12:19,371 --> 01:12:20,771
I feel pressured every time I enter the house.

2007
01:12:21,001 --> 01:12:22,801
Maybe he won't be able to go out at one point.

2008
01:12:26,711 --> 01:12:28,811
(Striding inside past the boxes)

2009
01:12:31,541 --> 01:12:32,551
(Putting them down)

2010
01:12:33,751 --> 01:12:35,780
(Filling the empty bookshelf...)

2011
01:12:35,781 --> 01:12:36,850
(with the books he got today)

2012
01:12:36,851 --> 01:12:39,790
The empty bookshelf bothered me every time.

2013
01:12:39,791 --> 01:12:40,920
Oh, the bookshelf there is empty,

2014
01:12:40,921 --> 01:12:42,420
- come to think of it. - Right.

2015
01:12:42,421 --> 01:12:43,661
You need to fill it.

2016
01:12:46,331 --> 01:12:48,291
(Grabbing)

2017
01:12:48,761 --> 01:12:50,301
Yes, let's open them.

2018
01:12:51,971 --> 01:12:54,471
(Opening the boxes without getting changed)

2019
01:12:56,041 --> 01:12:57,871
(Rustling)

2020
01:12:59,611 --> 01:13:01,370
- That looks like a bulb head. - A lamp.

2021
01:13:01,371 --> 01:13:02,411
(Blue bulb head)

2022
01:13:06,481 --> 01:13:07,581
(Putting it in)

2023
01:13:09,221 --> 01:13:10,251
What?

2024
01:13:10,381 --> 01:13:12,920
They sell a lampshade...

2025
01:13:12,921 --> 01:13:15,120
that you can insert in that lamp.

2026
01:13:15,121 --> 01:13:16,361
- A customized one? - Yes.

2027
01:13:16,891 --> 01:13:21,091
I wanted to use blue as an accent color.

2028
01:13:22,901 --> 01:13:25,230
(Art school graduate Hue...)

2029
01:13:25,231 --> 01:13:27,671
(is good at color coordination.)

2030
01:13:28,471 --> 01:13:31,371
(Interior taste that reflects his expertise as a former designer)

2031
01:13:34,241 --> 01:13:35,341
That's a lamp too.

2032
01:13:38,881 --> 01:13:40,981
I got blue items separately.

2033
01:13:43,051 --> 01:13:45,351
(Putting it in)

2034
01:13:47,721 --> 01:13:48,820
That's fascinating.

2035
01:13:48,821 --> 01:13:52,831
I wanted the stand to be blue like the lampshade.

2036
01:13:53,631 --> 01:13:54,930
(Among various colors, )

2037
01:13:54,931 --> 01:13:56,001
Goodness.

2038
01:13:56,331 --> 01:13:59,030
(Hue's personal squares are blue.)

2039
01:13:59,031 --> 01:14:01,801
It's nice to fill the space with decorative objects.

2040
01:14:02,871 --> 01:14:05,001
You have a keen eye for color.

2041
01:14:06,971 --> 01:14:08,941
- It's stylish. - It's pretty.

2042
01:14:11,041 --> 01:14:12,881
(Putting the blue frame on the floor)

2043
01:14:15,981 --> 01:14:18,551
(Keeping other objects in neat white except for accent objects)

2044
01:14:21,291 --> 01:14:25,121
I think it's important to decorate the space you're often in.

2045
01:14:25,491 --> 01:14:28,431
- That way, you grow fond of it. - That's right.

2046
01:14:32,931 --> 01:14:34,001
That's nice.

2047
01:14:34,131 --> 01:14:35,301
I like its mood.

2048
01:14:36,701 --> 01:14:39,171
(Heading to the kitchen after decorating)

2049
01:14:40,971 --> 01:14:43,481
(Rummaging through something without even getting changed)

2050
01:14:45,041 --> 01:14:46,481
(Pulling something out)

2051
01:14:47,511 --> 01:14:50,621
(Adding a handful of pasta noodles)

2052
01:14:52,121 --> 01:14:54,891
(Prepping cherry tomatoes as well)

2053
01:14:56,291 --> 01:14:58,391
Alexa, what's the time?

2054
01:14:58,861 --> 01:15:00,391
It's 6:07pm.

2055
01:15:02,631 --> 01:15:05,061
(Heading to the front door after checking the time)

2056
01:15:07,771 --> 01:15:08,841
What's that?

2057
01:15:09,271 --> 01:15:11,700
Yes, that's how you become when you live in Korea.

2058
01:15:11,701 --> 01:15:12,771
Getting quick deliveries.

2059
01:15:13,941 --> 01:15:15,071
Gosh, I'm so tired!

2060
01:15:16,141 --> 01:15:17,611
- There's another one? - What did he order?

2061
01:15:18,411 --> 01:15:21,280
The packages were piled up outside.

2062
01:15:21,281 --> 01:15:22,281
(There are still a lot of packages left.)

2063
01:15:22,681 --> 01:15:24,881
There's no end to opening packages.

2064
01:15:26,921 --> 01:15:28,991
(Hurrying)

2065
01:15:29,591 --> 01:15:31,861
It was my first time cooking at the new place,

2066
01:15:32,021 --> 01:15:34,630
so I had to order the things I needed one by one.

2067
01:15:34,631 --> 01:15:35,731
That's right.

2068
01:15:36,331 --> 01:15:38,531
Alexa, what's the time?

2069
01:15:39,701 --> 01:15:41,771
It's 6:15pm.

2070
01:15:43,841 --> 01:15:45,971
(He keeps checking the time while opening the packages.)

2071
01:15:47,211 --> 01:15:49,040
That takes up all the time.

2072
01:15:49,041 --> 01:15:51,381
It took really long to open everything.

2073
01:15:52,311 --> 01:15:56,221
(Rushing)

2074
01:15:57,951 --> 01:16:00,291
- Gosh, that looks fresh. - He makes it proper.

2075
01:16:01,921 --> 01:16:04,061
Alexa, what's the time?

2076
01:16:04,491 --> 01:16:06,091
It's 6:24pm.

2077
01:16:07,361 --> 01:16:09,431
(Quickly shaking the salad out of the bag)

2078
01:16:11,261 --> 01:16:13,531
- Oh, you had food delivered too. - Oh, it's sushi.

2079
01:16:13,631 --> 01:16:14,700
He's fast.

2080
01:16:14,701 --> 01:16:15,971
Someone must be coming.

2081
01:16:17,941 --> 01:16:19,010
He made a lot.

2082
01:16:19,011 --> 01:16:20,171
Someone must be coming.

2083
01:16:20,341 --> 01:16:22,441
- That's a lot for one. - Yes, it is.

2084
01:16:28,481 --> 01:16:29,581
Who could it be?

2085
01:16:30,051 --> 01:16:31,151
Who is it?

2086
01:16:31,781 --> 01:16:32,791
Who is it?

2087
01:16:33,451 --> 01:16:35,620
It's me.

2088
01:16:35,621 --> 01:16:37,320
- What? - Come in.

2089
01:16:37,321 --> 01:16:38,591
What? It's a woman.

2090
01:16:39,761 --> 01:16:41,560
(Smiling widely)

2091
01:16:41,561 --> 01:16:42,931
The door is open.

2092
01:16:44,161 --> 01:16:45,771
(Opening)

2093
01:16:46,101 --> 01:16:47,171
Hi.

2094
01:16:48,471 --> 01:16:49,840
- Hi. - Who are they?

2095
01:16:49,841 --> 01:16:51,540
- Why are you dressed like that? - Sorry.

2096
01:16:51,541 --> 01:16:53,210
- Will! - I knew it. It's Jin Joo.

2097
01:16:53,211 --> 01:16:54,710
- Park Jin Joo? - Oh, my.

2098
01:16:54,711 --> 01:16:55,781
(Songwriter Will Aronson and Actress Park Jin Joo)

2099
01:16:55,911 --> 01:16:57,540
Jin Joo is getting married.

2100
01:16:57,541 --> 01:16:58,541
- With who? - Really?

2101
01:16:58,542 --> 01:17:00,080
Who else? A man, probably.

2102
01:17:00,081 --> 01:17:01,511
- How would we know? - She's getting married?

2103
01:17:01,651 --> 01:17:04,521
The guests I invited for today's housewarming...

2104
01:17:04,651 --> 01:17:07,191
My partner who can't be separated from me...

2105
01:17:07,391 --> 01:17:09,761
and also my enemy, Will Aronson.

2106
01:17:09,861 --> 01:17:13,491
Actress Park Jin Joo, whom I've been working...

2107
01:17:13,591 --> 01:17:16,061
on a musical together for two years,

2108
01:17:16,161 --> 01:17:18,531
came to my housewarming, which I'm grateful for.

2109
01:17:19,131 --> 01:17:22,301
(Songwriter Will won the Tony Award with Hue.)

2110
01:17:23,141 --> 01:17:25,941
(Korean skills that surprised everyone)

2111
01:17:27,111 --> 01:17:28,440
(She drew much attention...)

2112
01:17:28,441 --> 01:17:29,781
(with her hyper-realistic acting of a nurse.)

2113
01:17:30,211 --> 01:17:31,380
(They've been working together since "Maybe Happy Ending.")

2114
01:17:31,381 --> 01:17:33,110
She's the lead of my musical.

2115
01:17:33,111 --> 01:17:34,251
That's right.

2116
01:17:34,381 --> 01:17:36,650
Will came to Korea to prepare...

2117
01:17:36,651 --> 01:17:38,680
"Maybe Happy Ending" in Korea with me.

2118
01:17:38,681 --> 01:17:40,250
- So he came also. - We see each other every day...

2119
01:17:40,251 --> 01:17:41,891
in the practice room, actually.

2120
01:17:42,051 --> 01:17:43,890
We're really comfortable with each other.

2121
01:17:43,891 --> 01:17:46,030
- You look really comfortable. - You become really close...

2122
01:17:46,031 --> 01:17:47,630
after doing a show together. You must know.

2123
01:17:47,631 --> 01:17:49,561
We become like family.

2124
01:17:49,861 --> 01:17:51,101
Goodness.

2125
01:17:51,261 --> 01:17:52,330
Wait.

2126
01:17:52,331 --> 01:17:53,401
Come on in.

2127
01:17:53,831 --> 01:17:55,101
- Hue. - Yes?

2128
01:17:55,271 --> 01:17:56,840
Aren't you dressed too nicely?

2129
01:17:56,841 --> 01:17:57,841
You think so?

2130
01:17:57,971 --> 01:17:59,540
I gave a lecture at a college.

2131
01:17:59,541 --> 01:18:00,610
- You had a lecture today? - Yes.

2132
01:18:00,611 --> 01:18:02,581
So I had to wear a tie too.

2133
01:18:02,781 --> 01:18:04,781
Are you good at cooking too?

2134
01:18:04,981 --> 01:18:07,050
It's pasta, and it's gluten-free.

2135
01:18:07,051 --> 01:18:08,411
You can eat it too, Will.

2136
01:18:08,611 --> 01:18:11,950
Will is allergic to flour. He's allergic to gluten.

2137
01:18:11,951 --> 01:18:14,651
- I was scooping it... - Oh, dear.

2138
01:18:15,221 --> 01:18:16,221
Look at him.

2139
01:18:16,921 --> 01:18:18,660
You came when I was plating it.

2140
01:18:18,661 --> 01:18:21,360
Since you guys are here anyway, I won't be pretentious.

2141
01:18:21,361 --> 01:18:22,930
- Let's scoop it from here. - Shall we?

2142
01:18:22,931 --> 01:18:23,931
Yes.

2143
01:18:24,331 --> 01:18:25,831
(Taking a glance)

2144
01:18:26,331 --> 01:18:27,930
(They naturally look around the house.)

2145
01:18:27,931 --> 01:18:29,001
Mother?

2146
01:18:30,601 --> 01:18:32,371
Hue, you're going to put these here for good, right?

2147
01:18:33,171 --> 01:18:36,311
(Magazines filled with Hue's face)

2148
01:18:36,541 --> 01:18:37,940
That's a nice magazine. They had us too.

2149
01:18:37,941 --> 01:18:39,480
- They embraced us. - That's right.

2150
01:18:39,481 --> 01:18:41,081
They embraced Jang Woo too.

2151
01:18:41,851 --> 01:18:45,821
(It wasn't easy to take his body profile photos.)

2152
01:18:46,321 --> 01:18:48,451
(They embraced all kinds of bodies.)

2153
01:18:48,751 --> 01:18:51,120
It left lasting memories for many people.

2154
01:18:51,121 --> 01:18:53,561
- That's right. - A kind-hearted magazine.

2155
01:18:53,761 --> 01:18:55,260
Until I find the right spot...

2156
01:18:55,261 --> 01:18:56,330
You'll leave them here...

2157
01:18:56,331 --> 01:18:57,401
- until you turn 50, right? - No.

2158
01:18:57,461 --> 01:18:59,000
"I'm so cool."

2159
01:18:59,001 --> 01:19:01,001
- "I'm the best. I'm Hue." - "I'm cool."

2160
01:19:01,431 --> 01:19:02,740
You're teasing me as soon as you're here.

2161
01:19:02,741 --> 01:19:05,140
I should continue cooking. You guys hang by yourselves.

2162
01:19:05,141 --> 01:19:07,671
But they turned out really nicely.

2163
01:19:08,341 --> 01:19:10,841
Hold on. I'm sorry. I'm almost done.

2164
01:19:10,941 --> 01:19:12,211
You should go in first.

2165
01:19:13,281 --> 01:19:15,381
- Gosh. - Oh, gosh.

2166
01:19:17,621 --> 01:19:18,950
The Tony Award is there.

2167
01:19:18,951 --> 01:19:21,451
Gosh, it's my first time seeing it.

2168
01:19:21,921 --> 01:19:23,060
- Can I touch it? - I also...

2169
01:19:23,061 --> 01:19:24,621
Can we touch it?

2170
01:19:24,961 --> 01:19:26,461
- He's not here. We can touch it. - Hue.

2171
01:19:27,661 --> 01:19:28,691
It's heavy, right?

2172
01:19:29,501 --> 01:19:30,631
Goodness.

2173
01:19:31,261 --> 01:19:32,331
Does it feel real?

2174
01:19:32,771 --> 01:19:34,470
- It still doesn't. - Still?

2175
01:19:34,471 --> 01:19:35,500
No.

2176
01:19:35,501 --> 01:19:36,871
Which year were you born, Will?

2177
01:19:37,041 --> 01:19:39,110
- I was born in 1981. - What was that?

2178
01:19:39,111 --> 01:19:40,170
You were born in 1981?

2179
01:19:40,171 --> 01:19:41,741
Why? Why is that funny?

2180
01:19:42,271 --> 01:19:43,540
I'm quite old, right?

2181
01:19:43,541 --> 01:19:45,111
- No. - I'm past my prime.

2182
01:19:45,211 --> 01:19:46,751
How does he know that expression?

2183
01:19:47,151 --> 01:19:49,250
- Who told him that? - It's such an advanced expression.

2184
01:19:49,251 --> 01:19:52,251
He learns weird expressions from somewhere and uses them on me.

2185
01:19:52,921 --> 01:19:55,320
Will said he had past his prime.

2186
01:19:55,321 --> 01:19:57,921
It's just the beginning for Will. He's not past his prime.

2187
01:19:58,221 --> 01:20:00,190
- Am I passing my heyday now? - Not at all.

2188
01:20:00,191 --> 01:20:02,330
Will, your prime is now.

2189
01:20:02,331 --> 01:20:03,401
It's now.

2190
01:20:04,501 --> 01:20:06,330
(Let's eat first.)

2191
01:20:06,331 --> 01:20:08,600
- Yes. - Since this looks the sloppiest,

2192
01:20:08,601 --> 01:20:10,301
- Not at all. - I'll have it.

2193
01:20:11,371 --> 01:20:13,310
Let's sit on the floor.

2194
01:20:13,311 --> 01:20:15,470
- Since I live alone, - You're right.

2195
01:20:15,471 --> 01:20:18,481
So I have only one chair for myself.

2196
01:20:19,211 --> 01:20:22,420
I'm sorry. I don't have any big tables at home, so...

2197
01:20:22,421 --> 01:20:24,380
- Not at all. Yes, it's fun. - This is more fun.

2198
01:20:24,381 --> 01:20:25,750
- It feels like a picnic. - Right.

2199
01:20:25,751 --> 01:20:27,020
It looks fun.

2200
01:20:27,021 --> 01:20:30,461
(Hue's one-pan pasta is done.)

2201
01:20:31,521 --> 01:20:33,560
You should get a foldable table or something.

2202
01:20:33,561 --> 01:20:34,960
If anyone drops that on the carpet...

2203
01:20:34,961 --> 01:20:36,631
- It'll get really awkward. - That's right.

2204
01:20:37,201 --> 01:20:38,661
And squashing anchovies on it.

2205
01:20:40,671 --> 01:20:42,100
They could squash them on the carpet as they sit.

2206
01:20:42,101 --> 01:20:44,070
- Cheers. Thanks for coming. - Cheers.

2207
01:20:44,071 --> 01:20:45,340
- Thank you for inviting us, Hue. - Not at all.

2208
01:20:45,341 --> 01:20:46,610
It's my first time eating the food you cooked.

2209
01:20:46,611 --> 01:20:49,811
You two are the first proper guests I've had over.

2210
01:20:49,941 --> 01:20:51,741
- Can I try it now? - Can I try it now?

2211
01:20:52,241 --> 01:20:53,980
- First time eating what you cooked. - I'm really worried.

2212
01:20:53,981 --> 01:20:55,150
I think it'll be really bad.

2213
01:20:55,151 --> 01:20:56,650
I made it after a really long time.

2214
01:20:56,651 --> 01:20:58,751
- It looks really good. - It's totally ruined.

2215
01:20:58,951 --> 01:21:00,551
(Exclaiming)

2216
01:21:01,451 --> 01:21:02,661
It's really good.

2217
01:21:02,961 --> 01:21:04,891
I like it. I love it.

2218
01:21:05,061 --> 01:21:06,990
- I'm not kidding. - I'm glad it suits his palate.

2219
01:21:06,991 --> 01:21:08,391
At least one of you likes it.

2220
01:21:09,261 --> 01:21:10,530
(Jin Joo takes a bite too.)

2221
01:21:10,531 --> 01:21:11,561
(Exclaiming)

2222
01:21:13,201 --> 01:21:14,531
- Hue. - I really like it.

2223
01:21:15,331 --> 01:21:16,941
I really like it.

2224
01:21:18,101 --> 01:21:21,771
Thank you for eating it so well. I didn't expect it.

2225
01:21:22,681 --> 01:21:25,111
- Hue, it's really good. - It's really good.

2226
01:21:26,211 --> 01:21:28,011
There's a lot more.

2227
01:21:28,851 --> 01:21:31,180
- That's good. - You can eat a lot.

2228
01:21:31,181 --> 01:21:32,651
- I can eat a lot. - You don't have to save it.

2229
01:21:32,991 --> 01:21:34,051
Okay.

2230
01:21:38,591 --> 01:21:40,691
- Gosh, he must've starved. - Goodness.

2231
01:21:42,531 --> 01:21:44,861
- He eats it like it's a snack. - Tell me about it.

2232
01:21:45,101 --> 01:21:46,771
It must suit his palate.

2233
01:21:48,731 --> 01:21:50,101
He scraped the plate clean.

2234
01:21:50,401 --> 01:21:51,870
Hue, thank you so much.

2235
01:21:51,871 --> 01:21:52,940
Thank you so much.

2236
01:21:52,941 --> 01:21:53,971
By the way,

2237
01:21:54,941 --> 01:21:58,540
Will will return to New York someday.

2238
01:21:58,541 --> 01:22:00,980
Hue, you will go back and forth, right?

2239
01:22:00,981 --> 01:22:04,080
Right. We're returning to New York right after the show.

2240
01:22:04,081 --> 01:22:05,121
Yes.

2241
01:22:05,181 --> 01:22:06,921
It makes me sad to think about parting with you two.

2242
01:22:07,721 --> 01:22:08,951
Will, you told me this.

2243
01:22:09,191 --> 01:22:11,590
That you think I will be a good elder.

2244
01:22:11,591 --> 01:22:12,991
- What's that supposed to mean? - That's...

2245
01:22:13,731 --> 01:22:14,790
- Will told me this. - I didn't mean me.

2246
01:22:14,791 --> 01:22:17,161
"Jin Joo, I think you'll be a good elder."

2247
01:22:17,831 --> 01:22:20,730
He said, "Jin Joo, I think you'll be a good elder."

2248
01:22:20,731 --> 01:22:23,400
I wanted to tell you that I wished...

2249
01:22:23,401 --> 01:22:25,840
we could be friends 10 or 20 years later.

2250
01:22:25,841 --> 01:22:28,141
- I see. You mean later. - I just meant...

2251
01:22:28,211 --> 01:22:30,011
"You'll be a good elder."

2252
01:22:30,641 --> 01:22:32,551
I think I get what he means.

2253
01:22:32,881 --> 01:22:34,880
But the word you used, "elder,"

2254
01:22:34,881 --> 01:22:37,721
made me look forward to getting older.

2255
01:22:38,081 --> 01:22:39,150
For the first time.

2256
01:22:39,151 --> 01:22:40,550
Then I'm glad.

2257
01:22:40,551 --> 01:22:41,591
(Touched)

2258
01:22:41,691 --> 01:22:43,520
- A good elder. - It's not easy to be one.

2259
01:22:43,521 --> 01:22:45,560
- A good elder. - There are times...

2260
01:22:45,561 --> 01:22:47,190
when the expressions they use when they're unsure...

2261
01:22:47,191 --> 01:22:48,961
- resonate with me more. - That's right.

2262
01:22:49,561 --> 01:22:52,970
Will, I think you know more vocabularies than us.

2263
01:22:52,971 --> 01:22:55,400
When you learned Korean for the first time,

2264
01:22:55,401 --> 01:22:56,971
didn't you say you learned...

2265
01:22:57,341 --> 01:22:58,440
- through songs? - Yes, I learned through songs.

2266
01:22:58,441 --> 01:23:00,011
- What kind of songs? - I learned through songs.

2267
01:23:00,141 --> 01:23:02,211
- Yes. - Which songs were they?

2268
01:23:02,411 --> 01:23:03,680
- "Aside." - "Aside."

2269
01:23:03,681 --> 01:23:05,080
- What? - Through a song called "Aside?"

2270
01:23:05,081 --> 01:23:06,350
- Really? - "Aside."

2271
01:23:06,351 --> 01:23:07,610
- "Aside." - "Aside."

2272
01:23:07,611 --> 01:23:09,281
- Through a song called "Aside?" - What?

2273
01:23:09,581 --> 01:23:10,721
- "Aside." - Really?

2274
01:23:11,151 --> 01:23:12,790
He sings that song every day.

2275
01:23:12,791 --> 01:23:14,550
- "Aside?" - Our song?

2276
01:23:14,551 --> 01:23:15,690
- Yes. - That's right.

2277
01:23:15,691 --> 01:23:19,360
He listened to SHINee's album every day...

2278
01:23:19,361 --> 01:23:20,730
and studied Korean.

2279
01:23:20,731 --> 01:23:21,760
- Really? - That's fascinating.

2280
01:23:21,761 --> 01:23:23,430
- That's amazing. - He's a total fan.

2281
01:23:23,431 --> 01:23:25,260
- You must feel proud. - I wasn't thinking much,

2282
01:23:25,261 --> 01:23:27,531
